    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Welsh settlement of Y Wladfa in Argentine Patagonia occupied a unique position\r\nwithin the colonial world. Y Wladfa was unique due to its complex and dynamic relationship\r\nwith persecution, domination, and colonialism. In a process that this essay will refer to as ‘dualcoloniality’, at differing periods in time, the people of Y Wladfa found themselves the victims of\r\ncolonization and acting as colonizers themselves, sometimes simultaneously. This capstone\r\nargues that in studying the case of Y Wladfa and the contradictions and ambiguities present in its\r\nformation, some of the problematic notions inherent in settler colonial studies can be identified\r\nand addressed. In examining the justifications for colonization, narratives of friendship and\r\nmoments of compromise, this capstone will demonstrate the need to shift settler colonialist\r\ntheory away from binary understandings colonial encounter and subjugation. This study of Y\r\nWladfa and its unique and ambiguous position, therefore, will inform studies on the legacy of the\r\ncolony in current day Argentina and the wider work of settler colonial studies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lewis, E. (2023). Y WLADFA: WELSH SETTLER COLONIALISM IN THE ARGENTINE NATIONAL PROJECT . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/tb182gd4257. https://doi.org/10.25740/tb182gd4257.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
